main='''<main id="main"><div class="atlas-breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Overview</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><a href="/evidence/">Evidence</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span aria-current="page">Calibration preparation</span></div><div class="page-intro"><div class="eyebrow">21 October 2026 Chicago · Calibration protocol 0.1</div><h1>Prepare the reference chain.<br>Keep unmeasured bounds open.</h1><p class="lede">An unexecuted calibration/preparation protocol now connects the twelve measurement requirements to witness records, timing and leakage feasibility.</p></div><aside class="callout"><strong>Proposed and unexecuted · Development — no registry entry · Physical promotion 0</strong><p>Every receipt is blank/pending. Independent review and all physical calibration remain open. No hardware experiment or data reacquisition is reported; the circuit candidate and EXIT-03 remain HOLD.</p></aside><div class="actions"><a class="button secondary" href="/sources/calibration-protocol-v0.1/PROTOCOL.md">Read the proposed protocol ↗</a><a class="text-link" href="/sources/calibration-protocol-v0.1/BOUND_TEMPLATES.json">Twelve blank bound receipts ↓</a></div><section class="chapter"><div class="chapter-label">01</div><div><h2>Timing feasibility is the first gate.</h2><p>The proposed 0.2 ns budget is split into four 50 ps shares: reference epoch, path skew, within-run variation and clock drift. The drift share over the 8.286 ms record implies about 6.03 parts per billion fractional clock error under the declared linear-drift model.</p><p>Repeated RMS jitter alone cannot supply the required deterministic bound. The protocol requires applicable reference uncertainty, before/after checks and justified stability across every displaced sampling interval.</p><p>A 1 ns timing alternative fits the mathematical total budget, but fails the current 0.2 ns per-role requirement. It remains a separately identified design alternative; no frozen contract is replaced.</p></div></section><section class="chapter"><div class="chapter-label">02</div><div><h2>A conditional tail witness has measurable targets.</h2><p>The proposed reference envelope has three lines at −25, 0 and +25 Hz with magnitudes 1/16, 3/4 and 1/16 V. Its in-band spectral variation is 7/8 V; a 1/1000 V tail leaves an amplitude floor of 0.624 V under the declared model.</p><p>A finite-mode theorem gives tail variation ≤√K times the sum of measured complex RMS and coefficient error, plus outside-model variation. The illustrative 100-mode witness needs 60 µV measured RMS, 20 µV total coefficient error and 200 µV outside-model variation to reach 1 mV.</p><p>Mode completeness, orthogonality, stationarity and outside-mode contamination remain independent premises. Source command values do not certify a nonlinear simulator’s output.</p><a class="text-link" href="/sources/calibration-protocol-v0.1/THEOREM.md">Finite-mode witness theorem →</a></div></section><section class="chapter"><div class="chapter-label">03</div><div><h2>Blank receipts cannot admit a run.</h2><p>Each bound template separates its required ceiling from its unmeasured value. Review and witness templates preserve apparatus/run, epoch, channel, raw-file hashes, methods, uncertainty and dependencies. All twelve blank-bound software controls return HOLD.</p><p>Next extend the finite-mode witness to frequency-offset and Gram-matrix uncertainty, so a monitor’s clock and off-grid effects have explicit tolerances. Full energy, absolute native support, primitive state clock and action calibration remain open.</p><a class="text-link" href="/sources/calibration-protocol-v0.1/REVIEW_TEMPLATE.json">Blank review receipt ↓</a><a class="text-link" href="/sources/calibration-protocol-v0.1/METHOD_WITNESS_TEMPLATE.json">Blank method/witness record ↓</a><a class="text-link" href="/sources/calibration-protocol-v0.1/VERIFICATION.json">Arithmetic and HOLD controls →</a><a class="text-link" href="/evidence/evidence-admission/">Evidence-admission contract →</a></div></section></main>'''
